# Intro
A single, fixed-mass star at the center of a uniform, periodic glass-IC gas
box, with photoelectric heating and LW/FUV hyperbolic propagation on
(photoionization and radiation pressure off). The propagation scheme is a
Cattaneo-type flux-relaxation system with a finite signal speed `c_hyp`, an
M1 closure and a flux limiter; it is not a screened-diffusion (Yukawa)
equation, and its steady state is not a single closed-form profile. Checks
the converged radial profile of the propagated FUV/LW
interstellar-radiation-field energy (`FUVSpecificEnergies`/
`LWSpecificEnergies`) against this scheme's own DISCRETE steady-state
prediction, computed on the run's own real particle positions and smoothing
lengths, via `isrf_hyperbolic_propagation_check.py`.

## Metallicity
The screening length comes from a dust opacity that this code scales with
metallicity relative to Grackle's solar value; at `Z=0` there is no dust
and nothing to screen, so `initial_metallicity` (env var, `run.sh`) must
stay above 0. It defaults to solar (`Z/Zsun=1`).

## Cooling
`run.sh` passes `--cooling` so `cooling_init()` resolves Grackle's
`chemistry_data` (including `local_dust_to_gas_ratio`, which the dust
opacity above depends on) even though gas thermodynamics play no role in
what this example measures. `GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging:
1` in `params.yml` then skips the per-particle cooling/heating update
itself, so the gas's thermal and dynamical state stays fixed and the
propagated field is isolated from any gas response.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-tracers=GEAR` is required: the tracers module registers the
`FUVSpecificEnergies`/`LWSpecificEnergies` snapshot output (the fields
themselves live on `struct part`'s `feedback_data`). The Grackle cooling
mode is not critical here (only `chemistry_data` needs resolving, not any
mode-gated species network).

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
Override any parameter via the matching env var -- gas density
(`gas_density`, atom/cm^3), gas particle mass (`gas_mass`, Msun), star mass
and type (`star_mass`, `star_type`), resolution level (`level`, N =
`(2**level)**3` gas particles), run duration and stepping (`time_end`,
`dt_max`, `delta_time`), metallicity (`initial_metallicity`), and thread
count (`n_threads`); see `run.sh` for the full list and defaults.
`run_name=<dir> ./run.sh` moves the run's outputs into `<dir>` once done, to
keep more than one run's outputs side by side.

# Checking
```
python3 isrf_hyperbolic_propagation_check.py
```
`run.sh` runs this automatically against its own final snapshot. It solves
this scheme's own discrete steady-state fixed point on the run's real
particle positions/smoothing lengths/densities/masses, using the same
pairwise operators, closure tensor and flux limiter the C code uses, and
then applies two pass/fail criteria:

- the radially-binned `u(r)` profile, simulation against that discrete
  prediction, bin by bin (`--tol`, default 0.15 on the median relative
  error);
- a total-amplitude identity that needs no fit and no assumed profile,
  `sum(m*u/lambda) = (1/c)*sum(weight*L*extinction)` (`--amp-tol`, default
  0.10).

It deliberately does NOT compare against a continuum profile: once the
smoothing length is not small compared to the screening length (true well
within this project's own production resolution range), the discrete
scheme's own steady state departs from any continuum profile in a
predictable way, and comparing against a continuum target reports a
spurious failure. It also no longer gates on a fitted screening length,
which is not a meaningful single number under this closure; the fit is
still printed, for continuity, as information only. The reduced flux `f`
realized in the compared range is printed too, so a reader can see whether
the run sits on the free-streaming or the diffusive branch. Produces a
comparison plot (`--output`, default
`isrf_hyperbolic_propagation_check.png`).
